to maybe share my story as well.

00:16:50.150 --> 00:16:53.370
&gt;&gt; Yes, I would second
everything that Catherine said.

00:16:53.370 --> 00:16:58.250
There are things that are sprinkled
throughout one's day at Stanford that can

00:16:58.250 --> 00:17:02.590
really give you the opportunity to
come outside your comfort zone.

00:17:02.590 --> 00:17:07.900
I distinctly remember the very
first class of managing groups and

00:17:07.900 --> 00:17:14.090
teams is when the teacher told us,
or the professor told us,

00:17:14.090 --> 00:17:17.910
all right, introduce yourself to
the person sitting next to you.

00:17:17.910 --> 00:17:20.003
And don't say hi, don't say how are you?

00:17:20.003 --> 00:17:25.102
But delve right into the question about
tell me the story behind your name.

00:17:25.102 --> 00:17:29.726
And I was just taken back a little bit,
because I'm thinking when I meet people,

00:17:29.726 --> 00:17:32.242
or are introduced, or get to know someone,

00:17:32.242 --> 00:17:35.170
maybe that's not a conversation
I comes up at all.

00:17:35.170 --> 00:17:39.285
But there are instances like
one day that I experienced,

00:17:39.285 --> 00:17:44.172
where they kind of have this way of
being able to kind of deep quickly and

00:17:44.172 --> 00:17:47.533
establish that that
trust in that community.

00:17:47.533 --> 00:17:51.836
And like Catherine said,
there's the opportunity where you

00:17:51.836 --> 00:17:55.402
have classmates that give a talk for
40 minutes.

00:17:55.402 --> 00:17:58.094
And some of them,
they're really deep and personal.

00:17:58.094 --> 00:18:02.266
And these are kind of opportunities
that you're thinking to yourself,

00:18:02.266 --> 00:18:03.518
in another setting,

00:18:03.518 --> 00:18:08.160
I don't know if I would have been able
to learn that much about somebody else.

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:12.819
And so I'm grateful that there's
these opportunities to kind of step

00:18:12.819 --> 00:18:15.477
outside the comfort zone at Stanford.

00:18:15.477 --> 00:18:17.170
&gt;&gt; Cool, so-
&gt;&gt; Can I just?

00:18:17.170 --> 00:18:19.670
I think another thing that helps you is,

00:18:19.670 --> 00:18:24.620
like a lot of MBA programs, there's this
policy of grade nondisclosure as well.

00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:29.020
So if you're looking to get out of your
comfort zone in a more academic practical

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:34.795
sense, it's pretty low risk to take
courses that really challenge you a lot.

00:18:34.795 --> 00:18:39.640
Because even if it is pushing kind of
yourself way past what you've ever done

00:18:39.640 --> 00:18:44.406
before, and you don't get the best
grade or the grade you were hoping for,

00:18:44.406 --> 00:18:46.234
it doesn't really matter.

00:18:46.234 --> 00:18:49.279
The course program is designed for

00:18:49.279 --> 00:18:54.851
you to be able to try those new things and
really push yourself.

00:18:54.851 --> 00:18:59.999
&gt;&gt; Great, and so question here, we
talked a little bit about the transition

00:18:59.999 --> 00:19:06.073
the application process, transition to the
GSB, a little bit about the experience.

00:19:06.073 --> 00:19:11.724
And now already starting
to think of the next steps.

00:19:11.724 --> 00:19:16.320
Can you guys talk a little bit
about the job hunt process?

00:19:17.960 --> 00:19:20.380
And the recruiting process,
what's that like?

00:19:20.380 --> 00:19:23.080
Especially if you happen to be considered,
and

00:19:23.080 --> 00:19:25.930
you're not sure what you want to do
just yet, what does that look like?

00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:28.961
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I can stat us off here.

00:19:31.092 --> 00:19:35.097
So there's a specific question here
from Ryan about recruiting process,

00:19:35.097 --> 00:19:37.780
how quickly does it start
upon arrival at the GSB?

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:41.250
And how do you engage with
that if you still are unsure?

00:19:42.280 --> 00:19:43.380
So for me, I was unsure.

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:45.870
And I actually started
kind of working backwards,

00:19:45.870 --> 00:19:50.295
I thought about things
I didn't want to do.

00:19:50.295 --> 00:19:53.200
And as I went to some of
the recruiting events and

00:19:53.200 --> 00:19:56.030
things that happened, I became clear I
didn't really want to do consulting,

00:19:56.030 --> 00:19:58.030
I didn't want to do some of
the traditional finance stuff.

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:01.390
Some of the big tech companies
weren't appealing to me, so

00:20:01.390 --> 00:20:02.980
I just kind of started limiting.

00:20:02.980 --> 00:20:07.340
I went from what's all possible to
then marking things off the list.

00:20:07.340 --> 00:20:11.540
And I didn't have an internship

00:20:11.540 --> 00:20:15.460
fully locked in till probably
spring quarter, and that was fine.

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:19.625
There's a lot of pressure here in the fall
if you're doing traditional recruiting and

00:20:19.625 --> 00:20:21.745
maybe every MBA to get
that locked in early.

00:20:21.745 --> 00:20:25.331
But many, many,
many people don't have internships or

00:20:25.331 --> 00:20:30.250
certainly real clarity about their
jobs until a few months before summer.

00:20:30.250 --> 00:20:34.473
And I guess one thing that's a little bit
specific to the GSB is there's an academic

00:20:34.473 --> 00:20:36.112
adjustment period in the fall.

00:20:36.112 --> 00:20:41.063
Which basically prevents
recruiters from harassing you or

00:20:41.063 --> 00:20:46.490
having events or
anything til Thanksgiving, I think, Will?

00:20:46.490 --> 00:20:48.520
It's eight weeks or six weeks.

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:50.920
So all of the first years
are basically on lock down.

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:55.942
And I found actually that,
that was great for the first month or so.

00:20:55.942 --> 00:20:59.204
because it allowed me still be here and
meet people and focus on school, and

00:20:59.204 --> 00:21:01.320
the move, and things like that.

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:06.711
But then I actually started kind of
getting antsy, I want to go explore now.

00:21:06.711 --> 00:21:08.456
But I thought it was a nice time,

00:21:08.456 --> 00:21:12.605
because then I couldn't expect myself
to also be doing the job hunting yet.

00:21:12.605 --> 00:21:16.959
Because technically it was against school
policy, and I wasn't getting emails or

00:21:16.959 --> 00:21:21.187
getting recruiter things or having events
every night I feel like I should be at,

00:21:21.187 --> 00:21:22.450
it was all kind of quiet.

00:21:23.940 --> 00:21:28.690
&gt;&gt; I can maybe add some points as well.

00:21:28.690 --> 00:21:35.302
Definitely second the academic adjustment
period, that's where I mentioned,

00:21:35.302 --> 00:21:41.166
I found that to be pretty helpful to
allow me to focus on one area at a time.

00:21:41.166 --> 00:21:45.036
[LAUGH] I think in the veteran
community this might echo or

00:21:45.036 --> 00:21:47.608
was a bit of a phased approach for me.

00:21:47.608 --> 00:21:52.491
And academic adjustment period was kind of
like a line where I was shifting my focus

00:21:52.491 --> 00:21:54.563
from academics until that ended.

00:21:54.563 --> 00:22:00.920
And then trying to blend in some
recruitment along with academics.

00:22:02.210 --> 00:22:07.600
One thing that I did before coming
to school was explore some of,

00:22:09.520 --> 00:22:12.670
on the more traditional side,
some companies and

00:22:12.670 --> 00:22:17.250
firms will have things that you
can either attend over the summer.

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:23.210
So if you have the opportunity to
take terminal leave or vacation or

00:22:23.210 --> 00:22:27.960
stop working with a little bit of
space before you start school,

00:22:27.960 --> 00:22:34.160
I think that was very helpful to
do formal and informal searching.

00:22:34.160 --> 00:22:39.970
So I did some research on the Internet,
I spoke with people in industries.

00:22:41.270 --> 00:22:44.810
And kind of started to hone the questions
that I wanted to ask people so

00:22:44.810 --> 00:22:47.250
I could really listen to their answers and

00:22:47.250 --> 00:22:50.720
see what resonated with me and
what I wanted to focus on.

00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:53.380
I ended up focusing on consulting.

00:22:54.820 --> 00:22:59.080
I went through a fairly large cycle,
including

00:22:59.080 --> 00:23:04.160
an internship at Goldman that kind
of directed me away from some

00:23:05.320 --> 00:23:09.190
other industries as well as
looking at stuff in tech.

00:23:10.856 --> 00:23:14.410
And I thought that
the recruitment process,

00:23:14.410 --> 00:23:20.430
when that probably more traditional
process started, it is pretty fast.

00:23:20.430 --> 00:23:24.832
So if you do think that that's what you
want to go down, or your research and

00:23:24.832 --> 00:23:28.460
reflection over the summer
starts putting you on that path.

00:23:28.460 --> 00:23:33.534
It's good to do a little bit of
the pre-work before that kicks off,

00:23:33.534 --> 00:23:39.959
because when academic adjustment period
ends, you're kind of in a massive sprint.

00:23:41.639 --> 00:23:46.649
From I think it was like
October through January,

00:23:46.649 --> 00:23:48.829
until final round
interviews were complete.

00:23:50.165 --> 00:23:56.660
Yeah, how did folks facilitate learning
about job opportunities or pivots?

00:23:56.660 --> 00:23:59.126
Did you use the Career Management Center?

00:23:59.126 --> 00:24:04.071
Did you network through events on campus,
through classmates or alumni?

00:24:04.071 --> 00:24:07.971
What were some of the different ways
that you've engaged with the recruiting

00:24:07.971 --> 00:24:08.514
process?

00:24:08.514 --> 00:24:12.097
&gt;&gt; We attended a lot of events
that were hosted on campus.

00:24:12.097 --> 00:24:16.130
But I also talked a lot to my classmates
because your classmates are going to

00:24:16.130 --> 00:24:20.821
have worked in all of these industries
that you're looking at, most likely.

00:24:20.821 --> 00:24:25.123
Including, you'll probably have classmates
that have founded their own companies in

00:24:25.123 --> 00:24:28.100
the past, and
have experienced entrepreneurship as well.

00:24:28.100 --> 00:24:33.350
So really almost anything that
you might be interested in,

00:24:33.350 --> 00:24:37.960
you can probably find a peer that has done
something like that and would be more than

00:24:37.960 --> 00:24:42.210
happy to sit down over coffee, or
take a walk and have a chat about it.

00:24:43.380 --> 00:24:47.370
&gt;&gt; I did a lot of some events,
a lot of brown bag lunches where companies

00:24:47.370 --> 00:24:50.664
will come and talk about themselves or
their industry.

00:24:50.664 --> 00:24:54.542
And usually there were some business
cards because they want an intern or two.

00:24:54.542 --> 00:24:59.375
And they'll literally be every lunch,
everyday of the week at lunch,

00:24:59.375 --> 00:25:02.283
there'll be multiple brown bag lunches.

00:25:02.283 --> 00:25:04.881
And usually you get free food and
you get to listen to the company.

00:25:04.881 --> 00:25:07.715
If you're interested, shake their
hands afterwards and talk to them,

00:25:07.715 --> 00:25:08.990
follow up email.

00:25:08.990 --> 00:25:10.260
So I did a lot of that.

00:25:10.260 --> 00:25:17.100
I also found I did a lot,
I held myself accountable to a couple

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:21.680
alumni reach outs per week, I think for
the first two quarters I was here.

00:25:21.680 --> 00:25:23.680
And I found that particularly effective,

00:25:23.680 --> 00:25:27.220
especially in the veteran community,
because it's a really small community.

00:25:27.220 --> 00:25:30.040
So there is one veteran
who graduated from here,

00:25:30.040 --> 00:25:32.050
probably ten years ago, I've never met.

00:25:33.060 --> 00:25:37.297
He was in the navy, I was in the army.

00:25:37.297 --> 00:25:42.162
And he has no connection to me other than
he went to the GSB ten years ago and now

00:25:42.162 --> 00:25:47.200
works as an investor in Texas for a big
Silicon Valley entrepreneur kind of guy.

00:25:47.200 --> 00:25:50.857
And not only did he take my first call,
but then he suggested a time to follow up

00:25:50.857 --> 00:25:54.372
next week, we had about a 45-minute call,
and I was out of questions.

00:25:54.372 --> 00:25:57.641
I didn't even know what's talking about
anymore, but he was just so happy to chat.

00:25:57.641 --> 00:26:01.996
And that kind of daisy chains onto
you're interested in this, hear me,

00:26:01.996 --> 00:26:04.275
you got to talk to this guy or
this guy on.

00:26:04.275 --> 00:26:10.390
And so I found the veterans alums to be
super helpful, small but really impactful.

00:26:12.970 --> 00:26:17.330
&gt;&gt; Cool, and we're getting
closer this part of the section.

00:26:17.330 --> 00:26:20.350
So if we can close out,
I have one question that actually

00:26:20.350 --> 00:26:25.540
encapsulates a couple of different things
folks have been asking in the Q&amp;A box.

00:26:25.540 --> 00:26:31.350
If you could share something like a piece
of advice, either with applicant you or

00:26:31.350 --> 00:26:36.160
like MBA one year you when you were just
starting, what would that advice be?

00:26:37.630 --> 00:26:43.003
&gt;&gt; Yeah, for MBA one me, I would say
that if you haven't heard already,

00:26:43.003 --> 00:26:47.393
there is this notion of the FOMO,
or fear of missing out.

00:26:47.393 --> 00:26:51.438
There are going to be a lot
of events going on on campus.

00:26:51.438 --> 00:26:56.202
You got your classwork, you got
these career events, you have clubs.

00:26:56.202 --> 00:27:00.716
And then you have just the natural
tendency of classmates just wanting to

00:27:00.716 --> 00:27:04.800
hang out, grab a drink, or
go for a walk, or whatnot.

00:27:04.800 --> 00:27:10.297
And it can very easily
overwhelm you very quickly.

00:27:10.297 --> 00:27:15.950
And the thing is, just treat it like
you've treated anything else in the past.

00:27:15.950 --> 00:27:19.170
It's something that,
it's okay to say no to certain events,

00:27:19.170 --> 00:27:21.470
it's okay to take certain time.

00:27:21.470 --> 00:27:28.906
There's not this notion that everyone's
doing something at any given time.

00:27:28.906 --> 00:27:34.544
This is your journey, so make sure you
take time to do what you want to do.

00:27:34.544 --> 00:27:39.472
And if you need some time off
to make sure you do that,

00:27:39.472 --> 00:27:44.859
make this experience how you
want this experience to be.

00:27:44.859 --> 00:27:48.981
[MUSIC]

